(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-quiz"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In which patient are modafinil and armodafinil used with caution?","choices":["One with liver disease","One with allergic rhinitis","One with supine-position events","One with hypothyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These stimulants need caution in patients with cardiovascular, hepatic or psychiatric conditions, so liver disease calls for care. They also have many potential drug interactions, and elderly patients receive lower doses. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 11, 12"},{"q":"Which level falls in untreated obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Cortisol","C-reactive protein","Amyloid","Growth hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Among the endocrine effects of untreated obstructive sleep apnea, growth hormone and prolactin decrease, while C-reactive protein, cortisol and amyloid increase. Proinflammatory cytokines rise as well and improve with continuous positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 21, 22"},{"q":"If continuous positive airway pressure fails in central sleep apnea, which therapy is considered next?","choices":["Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Bilevel positive airway pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After treating the underlying cause, continuous positive airway pressure is considered first; if it fails, adaptive servo-ventilation follows. It monitors breathing patterns in real time and delivers or adjusts pressure as needed, but it is not safe for every patient. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 26, 33"},{"q":"For a patient who cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure, which surgical option is limited to mild obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Palatal implants","Radiofrequency ablation","Tracheostomy","Maxillomandibular advancement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Palatal implants are used in patients with mild obstructive sleep apnea who cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure. Radiofrequency ablation reaches a little further, to mild to moderate disease in the same intolerant patients. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which pairing of cause and first management step in central sleep apnea is correct?","choices":["Opioid use: keep opioids, add pressure","High altitude: oral appliance first","High altitude: descend from altitude","Heart failure: begin servo-ventilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea treatment starts with the underlying cause: descend from altitude at high altitude, reduce opioid medications when they are the cause, and optimize heart failure therapy in heart failure. Continuous positive airway pressure is then considered first. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 26"},{"q":"What body mass index meets the obesity criterion for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Above 35 kg/m2","Above 40 kg/m2","Above 30 kg/m2","Above 25 kg/m2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obesity for this diagnosis means a body mass index above 30, together with awake arterial carbon dioxide above 45 mmHg and exclusion of other causes. The syndrome becomes more likely as body mass index increases, and patients are typically obese and hypersomnolent. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 17, 28, 30, 32"},{"q":"Which referral is part of patient education for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Neurologist","Sleep physician","Cardiologist","Gastroenterologist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for obstructive sleep apnea includes referral to a sleep physician and proper counseling, alongside risk modification: diet and exercise for weight loss and avoiding alcohol and other sedatives. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 15, 19"},{"q":"What is first-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Bariatric surgery as initial therapy","Weight-loss medications from the start","Weight loss plus positive airway pressure","Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy is a weight loss program plus positive airway pressure, continuous or bilevel, within a multidisciplinary approach that adds lifestyle modification: diet changes, exercise and behavioral modifications. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which second-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome is very effective?","choices":["Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Bariatric surgery for weight loss","Adaptive servo-ventilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For patients who fail or do not tolerate first-line therapy, tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation is very effective. Bariatric surgery may be helpful, and medications are reserved for those who fail diet and exercise measures. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 26, 33"},{"q":"What is the leading cause of death in obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Cardiovascular disease","Hypercapnic respiratory failure","Motor vehicle crashes","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Untreated, the syndrome tends to progress, and many patients develop pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure, with high mortality and morbidity overall. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 20, 30, 35"},{"q":"What causes the breathing events of obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Loss of brainstem drive","Excess sensitivity to carbon dioxide","Weak inspiratory muscles","Upper airway narrowing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive sleep apnea occurs because the upper airway narrows, from excessive soft tissue or structural abnormalities such as macroglossia, retrognathia or enlarged tonsils. The airway collapses while the patient still tries to breathe. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 24, 29"},{"q":"On the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, what body mass index scores a point?","choices":["More than 35 kg/m2","More than 30 kg/m2","More than 40 kg/m2","More than 45 kg/m2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The body mass index item scores a point at more than 35 kg/m2. A body mass index above 35 also joins a high-risk combination: yes to 2 or more of the first four questions plus a body mass index above 35 places a person at high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In which setting has the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire been used?","choices":["Community-wide screening","Confirming the diagnosis","Grading disease severity","Preoperative evaluation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The questionnaire has been used in preoperative evaluation to find people without a known diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea who may be at risk for perioperative complications. It has the highest sensitivity among the questionnaires. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 16"},{"q":"In a split-night polysomnogram, what follows the first 2 hours of recorded sleep when obstructive sleep apnea is significant?","choices":["Continuous nocturnal carbon dioxide monitoring","A second baseline recording night","Continuous positive airway pressure titration","Adaptive servo-ventilation trial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A split-night study records the first 2 hours of sleep to determine severity, then, if obstructive sleep apnea is significant, titrates continuous positive airway pressure: finding the exact air pressure needed to keep the airway open. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 26, 32"},{"q":"Untreated obstructive sleep apnea triples the risk of which condition?","choices":["Hypertension","Driving accidents","Insulin resistance","Gout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries 3 times the risk of hypertension, and poorly controlled hypertension is itself a clue to the disorder. Blood pressure also becomes more variable, and sympathetic tone rises. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 18, 20, 21"},{"q":"For which people does the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire have some value as a screening tool?","choices":["People already diagnosed with the disorder","Adults at routine yearly wellness visits","Symptomatic people in high-risk settings","Asymptomatic adults in the community"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The tool has some value in highly symptomatic patients in high-risk settings, such as preoperative evaluation. It cannot be used as a true, accurate screening tool in asymptomatic patients, and questionnaires should not routinely screen the community. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Fat carried at the hips, not the waist","Mild obstructive sleep apnea","Preserved lung function","Reduced inspiratory muscle strength"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors include reduced inspiratory muscle strength, central obesity (a high waist-to-hip ratio), reduced lung function due to obesity and severe obstructive sleep apnea. The syndrome becomes more likely as body mass index increases. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 29, 30"},{"q":"Which is the most common form of sleep-disordered breathing?","choices":["Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Obstructive sleep apnea","Central sleep apnea","Sleep-related hypoventilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive sleep apnea is the most common form of sleep-disordered breathing, caused by narrowing of the upper airway from excessive soft tissue or structural abnormalities. Its prevalence increases with age. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 24, 28, 30"},{"q":"Which patient with suspected obstructive sleep apnea should have a polysomnogram instead of an at-home test?","choices":["One with loud habitual snoring","One with witnessed apneas","One with suspected moderate disease","One on long-term opioids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Long-term opioid use is one of the conditions that call for a polysomnogram rather than a home kit, along with significant cardiopulmonary disease, neuromuscular weakness, awake hypoventilation or hypoxemia, stroke and severe insomnia. Opioids are also a risk factor for central sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 24"},{"q":"For obesity hypoventilation syndrome with sleep-related hypoventilation, which pressure therapy is paired with weight loss?","choices":["Continuous positive airway pressure","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Bilevel positive airway pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When obesity hypoventilation syndrome coexists with sleep-related hypoventilation, first-line therapy pairs a weight loss program with bilevel positive airway pressure. When it coexists with obstructive sleep apnea, continuous positive airway pressure is used instead. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 26, 30, 33"},{"q":"Which description defines a hypopnea?","choices":["Slowed, shallow, restricted breathing","Airflow drop of 90% or more","Hyperventilation alternating with apnea","Hypoventilation while awake"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A hypopnea is slowed, shallow, restricted breathing occurring in episodes of 10 seconds or longer, repeatedly during sleep. Hypopneas join apneas in the apnea-hypopnea index, the count of both per hour of total sleep time. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 9, 28"},{"q":"In an obese patient, at or below what unexplained awake room-air oxygen saturation should obesity hypoventilation syndrome be screened for?","choices":["80% or less","88% or less","94% or less","97% or less"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An obese patient, with or without obstructive apnea, should be screened when an unexplained awake room-air oxygen saturation is 94% or less, or the overnight lowest saturation is under 80%. Unexplained exertional dyspnea, right-heart signs, facial plethora and raised bicarbonate also qualify. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"When is the 3 to 6 times higher risk of sudden cardiac death in untreated obstructive sleep apnea concentrated?","choices":["After large meals","At night","During exercise","After waking"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries a 3 to 6 times higher risk of sudden cardiac death at night, while the breathing events occur during sleep. Mortality is also driven by heart failure and by 2.5 times the driving accidents. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 18, 19"},{"q":"Which describes hospital care in obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Less long-term care at discharge","Less use of health care resources","More intensive care and intubation","Fewer hospital admissions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalization rates are higher in obesity hypoventilation syndrome, and patients are more likely to need intensive care, intubation or long-term care at discharge. They also have worse health status and use more health care resources. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which symptoms persist in elderly patients with obstructive sleep apnea and improve with continuous positive airway pressure?","choices":["Exertional dyspnea and plethora","Drowsiness and nocturia","Insomnia and nocturnal dyspnea","Pedal edema and hepatomegaly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mortality from obstructive sleep apnea is lower in the elderly because they compensate, yet drowsiness and nocturia persist, and both improve with continuous positive airway pressure. Nocturia is also a core history finding of the disorder at any age. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 22, 25, 31"},{"q":"A woman answers yes to loud snoring, to being treated for high blood pressure and to being older than 50, and no to daytime tiredness and observed apneas; her body mass index is 33 kg/m2 and her shirt collar is 15 inches. What is her STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) total?","choices":["3","4","5","6"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + pressure 1 + age older than 50 1 = 3; tiredness, observed apnea, body mass index (33 is not above 35), neck (15 is under 16 inches) and gender (female) score 0. A total of 3 is intermediate risk, and no combination rule applies. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"On the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which set of yes answers places a person at high risk despite a total below 5?","choices":["Body mass index over 35 and male gender","Snoring, tiredness and a 16-inch neck","Snoring, male gender and age over 50","Male gender, age over 50 and 16-inch neck"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring and tiredness are 2 of the first four questions, and a neck of 16 inches (40 cm) or larger completes a high-risk combination. The total is only 3, yet the person is high risk, as with 2 or more of the first four plus male gender or a body mass index above 35. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Without a qualifying symptom, how many predominantly obstructive respiratory events per hour of sleep confirm obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["5 or more","10 or more","15 or more","20 or more"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fifteen or more predominantly obstructive events per hour confirm obstructive sleep apnea on their own, provided the findings are not explained by another condition, medication or substance. With 5 or more events, a qualifying symptom is also required. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What drives the unstable breathing pattern of central sleep apnea?","choices":["Decreased sensitivity to carbon dioxide","Upper airway collapse during effort","Decreased intrathoracic pressure","Increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In central sleep apnea the brainstem stops sending signals to breathe: increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide creates an unstable pattern of hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Airflow stops because effort is lacking, not because the airway collapses. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 19, 24"},{"q":"Which surgical procedure is a cure for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Tracheostomy","Palatal implants","Radiofrequency ablation","Bariatric surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tracheostomy is a cure for obstructive sleep apnea: it bypasses the site of upper airway obstruction, so air no longer passes the collapsing segment. Other surgery, such as radiofrequency ablation or palatal implants, targets milder disease in patients who cannot tolerate pressure therapy. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 13, 33"}]};
